1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
So, let's talk about the War on Drugs.

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:07,800
Today we're going to dig into the playbook behind it, using some pretty revealing documents

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:13,040
and interviews to see how this one political strategy really changed the course of a nation's

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:14,040
history.

5
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:18,640
Let's kick things off with a quote, a really stunning admission from deep inside the Nixon

6
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:19,740
White House.

7
00:00:19,740 --> 00:00:23,760
This one line pretty much reframes the entire story of the War on Drugs.

8
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,040
So you gotta ask, right?

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:26,040
Who said this?

10
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:28,200
And what in the world were they lying about?

11
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:30,480
Okay, so let's dive into this.

12
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:35,720
The crazy thing is, this quote came out way back in a 1994 interview, but it just kind

13
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:37,220
of sat there.

14
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:42,560
It flew completely under the radar for more than 20 years until, boom, a 2016 article

15
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:46,840
in Harper's Magazine brought it to light and it just exploded.

16
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:50,520
And we're not talking about some low-level staffer here, nope.

17
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:53,200
The person who said this was John Ehrlichman.

18
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:58,520
He was one of President Nixon's most trusted advisors, a key architect of domestic policy.

19
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:00,560
So when he talks, you listen.

20
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:02,600
And his confession, wow.

21
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:07,220
It gives us this really chilling peek into the cold, hard political math that was going

22
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:08,960
on behind the scenes.

23
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,480
The journalist who did the interview said Ehrlichman was just brutally honest.

24
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:14,000
He basically laid it all out.

25
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,560
The Nixon campaign, he said, had two enemies, the anti-war left and black people.

26
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:22,440
And while you can't just make it illegal to be against a war or to be black, so they

27
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:23,560
had to get creative.

28
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:27,960
They needed another way to go after those communities to disrupt and dismantle them.

29
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:30,560
And this is the playbook he described.

30
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:32,960
It's pretty methodical.

31
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:35,560
Step one, you create a public association.

32
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:39,560
You link the hippies with marijuana and you link black people with heroin.

33
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:43,760
Step two, you make both those things super illegal, criminalize them heavily.

34
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:47,000
And once you've done that, you've got the perfect excuse to disrupt their communities,

35
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,000
right?

36
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,160
You can raid their homes, arrest their leaders, and, in his own words, vilify them night

37
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,400
after night on the evening news.

38
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:55,680
It was a political weapon.

39
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:56,680
He admitted it.

40
00:01:56,680 --> 00:02:00,640
So this whole strategy, targeting specific groups of people without actually naming

41
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:02,800
them, this wasn't just a one-time thing.

42
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:07,360
Oh no, it actually became the foundation for a whole new kind of political language.

43
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:11,440
You could call it an abstract attack, one that was specifically designed to hide what

44
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:12,960
it was really about.

45
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:13,960
And that brings us to this.

46
00:02:13,960 --> 00:02:19,320
Well, it's a pretty infamous interview from 1981 with a guy named Lee Atwater, who was

47
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,520
a top Republican strategist.

48
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:22,880
But he just spelled it out.

49
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:27,320
He explained exactly how political messaging had to change once you couldn't use overtly

50
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:30,120
racist language anymore because it would backfire.

51
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:31,120
The solution?

52
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,120
Coded phrases.

53
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:36,560
Atwater literally described it as a three-step evolution.

54
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:38,960
You start out using explicit slurs.

55
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:42,360
Then, when that stops being socially acceptable, you pivot.

56
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:45,560
You switch to coded phrases, like states' rights.

57
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:50,040
And then, the final step, you move to something that sounds totally neutral, like abstract

58
00:02:50,080 --> 00:02:53,240
economic policies, things like cutting taxes.

59
00:02:53,240 --> 00:02:57,680
But as Atwater himself pointed out, a byproduct of those policies is that blacks get hurt

60
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,000
worse than whites.

61
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,520
So the language gets cleaner, more abstract, but the intended effect?

62
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:04,240
That stays the same.

63
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:06,280
You've probably heard the name for this tactic.

64
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:07,920
It's called a dog whistle.

65
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:09,960
You know, like a whistle-only dogs can hear?

66
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:14,200
It's a political message that sounds totally innocent to the general population, but for

67
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:18,480
a specific, targeted audience, the real meaning comes through loud and clear.

68
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:22,600
So in this context, something like states' rights wasn't just about states' rights.

69
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:27,320
It became a dog whistle for pushing back against federal civil rights enforcement.

70
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:31,920
Now as shocking as all this is, the Nixon administration didn't just invent this playbook

71
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:32,920
out of thin air.

72
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:33,920
Not at all.

73
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:36,280
There was a clear historical precedent for this.

74
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:37,960
It goes back decades.

75
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:39,040
So let's rewind.

76
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,060
Let me introduce you to a man named Harry Anslinger.

77
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:47,000
This guy was basically the nation's first drug czar, way back in the 1930s.

78
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,520
And he led America's first big war on drugs, and his target was almost entirely marijuana.

79
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:58,280
And Anslinger's campaign, well, it was fueled by some just blatantly racist rhetoric.

80
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:00,560
He really didn't even try to hide his motivations.

81
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:05,160
He was constantly and directly linking drug use to what he saw as a threat to the existing

82
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:06,160
racial hierarchy.

83
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:12,320
I mean, his official justifications were this toxic cocktail of racial and cultural fear.

84
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:16,720
He just relentlessly tied marijuana to minority groups, and also to cultural things he didn't

85
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,920
like, like jazz and swing music.

86
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:22,680
The whole campaign was framed as this urgent threat to white society.

87
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:24,960
Okay, so we've seen the playbook.

88
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:26,120
We've seen the language.

89
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:27,960
But what were the real world consequences?

90
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:32,120
What actually happened because of this strategy from Anslinger all the way up through Nixon

91
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:33,120
and beyond?

92
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:36,680
Well, the data, it paints a pretty devastating picture.

93
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:38,360
Just look at this timeline.

94
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:39,840
It's undeniable.

95
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:45,080
The moment President Nixon officially declared a war on drugs back in 1971, the U.S. prison

96
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:47,560
population just starts to skyrocket.

97
00:04:47,560 --> 00:04:50,440
It's a climb unlike anything else in modern history.

98
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:55,520
We're talking from around 300,000 people back then to well over 2 million today.

99
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,720
And what was driving this explosion in the prison population?

100
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,920
Overwhelmingly, it was one thing.

101
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:01,920
Drug offenses.

102
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:07,160
The number of people in prison or jail for a drug crime went up by an incredible 1,100

103
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:11,600
percent after the war on drugs really ramped up in the 1980s.

104
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:14,200
Just think about that number for a second.

105
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:19,520
And as you can probably guess, this massive increase was not felt equally across the board.

106
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:20,960
Not even close.

107
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:24,040
Black Americans make up about 13 percent of the U.S. population.

108
00:05:24,040 --> 00:05:27,880
But if you look at the historical data, they've accounted for nearly 60 percent of the people

109
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,400
in state prisons for drug offenses.

110
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:32,020
So what explains that?

111
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:35,040
How is that massive disparity even possible?

112
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,040
Well here's the thing.

113
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:38,080
Decades of research have looked into this.

114
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,320
And it's not because of different rates of drug use.

115
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,520
The data is crystal clear on this.

116
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:47,640
White and black Americans use and sell illicit drugs at almost identical rates.

117
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:50,960
The difference, the real difference, is in enforcement.

118
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:56,080
It's about who gets stopped, who gets searched, and ultimately, who gets arrested.

119
00:05:56,080 --> 00:06:01,120
So all of this brings us full circle, right back to the legacy of this political playbook

120
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:03,680
and the kind of language that it created.

121
00:06:03,680 --> 00:06:06,840
The pattern becomes pretty clear when you lay it all out like this.

122
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:11,320
When one form of direct discrimination becomes socially unacceptable, new, more abstract

123
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:16,320
language is created to justify policies that, well, they produce a very similar result.

124
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:20,080
In this case, the phrase, tough on crime, effectively stepped in to do the political

125
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:23,240
work that tough on segregation just couldn't do anymore.

126
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:27,480
You know, understanding this history isn't just about memorizing facts and dates.

127
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:32,040
It's really about learning to see the systems that are at play behind the headlines, behind

128
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:33,040
the political rhetoric.

129
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:35,120
There's actually a term for this.

130
00:06:35,120 --> 00:06:38,940
Some sociologists call it developing a critical consciousness.

131
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:43,180
As one scholar put it so well, the language just shifted.

132
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:48,180
Arguments about black criminality simply replaced the older arguments about black inferiority.

133
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:52,100
It allowed for a similar outcome, but it was all done under this much more socially acceptable

134
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:54,700
banner of just being tough on crime.

135
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:58,340
And that really leaves us with a final thought to chew on.

136
00:06:58,340 --> 00:07:04,100
This playbook, this idea of using coded, abstract language to target a specific group, it doesn't

137
00:07:04,100 --> 00:07:05,380
just live in history books.

138
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:09,860
It's a powerful and really persistent political tool.

139
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:14,540
So the real question for all of us is, now that you know the playbook, where do you see

140
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:15,500
it being used today?

